What is the significance of axillary lymph node status in primary breast cancer?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The status of axillary lymph nodes is crucial in determining the course of early-stage breast cancer treatment. Axillary lymph nodes are small, oval-shaped organs that filter blood and urine, and they are responsible for filtering the fluid from the breast tissue. If breast cancer spreads to the axillary lymph nodes, it is called axillary spread or adjuvant therapy. This is because the cancer has spread beyond the breast tissue and may spread to other parts of the body, increasing the risk of cancer spreading (metamination).

Axillary lymph node status is evaluated by a biopsy of the axillary lymph nodes, either during the initial breast cancer treatment (neomamnodeectomy) or after the breast tissue is removed (lumectomy). The biopsy is usually done to determine the stage of the cancer and to help guide treatment planning.

Axillary lymph node status is important because it helps determine the stage of the cancer, which in turn influences the type and extent of treatment needed. For example, if the axillary lymph nodes are negative (no cancer cells are found), the cancer is less aggressive and may not require adjuvant therapy. However, if the axillary lymph nodes are positive (cancer cells are found), the cancer is more aggressive and may require adjuvant therapy, such as chemotherapy, hormone therapy, or targeted therapy.

In summary, the status of axillary lymph nodes in patients with early-stage breast cancer is important for determining the stage of the cancer and guiding treatment planning. If the axillary lymph nodes are negative, the cancer is less aggressive and may not require adjuvant therapy. If the axillary lymph nodes are positive, the cancer is more aggressive and may require adjuvant therapy to prevent the cancer from spreading.
