","The definition of whole-tissue oxygen delivery is $\boxed{\text{the total amount of oxygen delivered to all tissues in the body per unit time.}}$ It is a critical physiological parameter that reflects the efficiency of the respiratory and circulatory systems in delivering oxygen to the body's cells for energy production. It is calculated using the following formula:

\[
\text{Whole-tissue oxygen delivery} = \text{VOO} = \text{VO} \times \text{VO/V̇I}
\]

where:
- **VOO** is the whole-tissue oxygen uptake (ml/min)
- **VO** is the volume of oxygen consumed (ml/min)
- **V̇I** is the volume of inspired air (ml/min)

This formula is derived from the Fick equation, which relates the rate of gas transfer (VOO) to the partial pressure gradient of the gas (VO vs. V̇I) and the surface area and permeability of the tissue.
