","1. **Calculate the equivalents of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) in the 10.0 L of 2.00 N H\(_2\)SO\(_4\):**
   \[
   \text{Equivalents} = \text{Normality} \times \text{Volume (L)} = 2.00 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{L} = 20.0 \, \text{eq}
   \]
   Since H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) is diprotic, 1 mole provides 2 equivalents. Thus, the 10.0 L of 2.00 N H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) contains 10.00 moles of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\).

2. **Calculate the total equivalents of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) required in 20.0 L of 3.00 M H\(_2\)SO\(_4\):**
   \[
   \text{Equivalents} = \text{Molarity} \times \text{Volume (L)} = 3.00 \, \text{M} \times 20.0 \, \text{L} = 60.0 \, \text{eq}
   \]
   Since H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) is diprotic, 60.0 equivalents correspond to 30.0 moles of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\).

3. **Determine the amount of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) to be added from the 6.00 M solution:**
   \[
   \text{Moles of H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) to add} = 30.0 \, \text{moles} - 10.00 \, \text{moles} = 20.0 \, \text{moles}
   \]

4. **Calculate the volume of 6.00 M H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) required:**
   \[
   \text{Volume (L)} = \frac{\text{Moles}}{\text{Molarity}} = \frac{20.0 \, \text{moles}}{6.00 \, \text{M}} = 3.33 \, \text{L}
   \]

**Final Answer:**
The volume of 6.00 M H\(_2\)SO\(_4\) to be added is \(\boxed{3.33 \, \text{L}}\).
