Passing \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas into a mixture of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions in an acidified aqueous solution precipitates which sulfides?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The solubility of sulfides varies with the metal ion. Passing \( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) gas into an acidified aqueous solution of \( \text{Mn}^{2+}, \text{Ni}^{2+}, \text{Cu}^{2+}, \) and \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) ions will precipitate the following sulfides:  
- \( \text{MnS} \) (Manganese(II) sulfide)  
- \( \text{NiS} \) (Nickel(II) sulfide)  
- \( \text{HgS} \) (Mercury(II) sulfide)  

The solubility order of these sulfides is \( \text{MnS} > \text{NiS} > \text{HgS} \), meaning \( \text{MnS} \) is the least insoluble and \( \text{HgS} \) is the most insoluble.  

**Explanation:**  
\( \text{H}_2\text{S} \) dissociates in water to form \( \text{S}^{2-} \) ions, which react with the metal ions to form sulfides. The solubility of the sulfide depends on the charge density of the metal ion and the size of the sulfide ion.  

- \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \) and \( \text{Ni}^{2+} \) form sulfides that are less insoluble than \( \text{Cu}^{2+} \), so they will precipitate.  
- \( \text{Hg}^{2+} \) forms a very insoluble sulfide, \( \text{HgS} \), and will precipitate readily.  

Thus, the correct answer is \( \boxed{\text{MnS}, \text{NiS}, \text{HgS}} \).
