","Absolute asymmetric synthesis is a type of asymmetric synthesis where the reaction produces a single enantiomer (a single stereoisomer) with 100% enantiomeric excess (ee). This means that the reaction produces only one stereoisomer of the product, with no contamination from the other enantiomer. This is the highest level of enantioselectivity possible in asymmetric synthesis.

The difference between absolute asymmetric synthesis and other types of asymmetric synthesis lies in the level of enantioselectivity and the method used to achieve it. In other types of asymmetric synthesis, the reaction may produce a high enantiomeric excess (e.g., 90% ee), but it still contains a small amount of the other enantiomer. Asymmetric synthesis can be achieved through various methods, such as the use of chiral catalysts, chiral auxiliaries, or enzymatic reactions.

In contrast, absolute asymmetric synthesis requires a more defined and controlled mechanism to guarantee the formation of only one enantiomer. This can be achieved by using a chiral catalyst or reagent that reacts with the substrate in a highly enantioselective manner, or by exploiting the inherent chirality of the starting material to control the stereochemical outcome of the reaction.

An example of absolute asymmetric synthesis is the synthesis of (R)-1-phenylpyrrolidine using the Sharpless asymmetric epoxidation reaction. In this reaction, a chiral rhodium complex is used as a catalyst to selectively epoxidze the double bond in a substrate, leading to the formation of a single enantiomer of the epoxide. The enantioselectivity of the reaction is controlled by the chirality of the rhodium complex, which selectively reacts with one enantiomer of the substrate to form the desired product. The reaction proceeds with an ee of 90-95%, which is considered high enantioselectivity, but it is not considered absolute asymmetric synthesis because it still contains a small amount of the opposite enantiomer.

In summary, while all asymmetric synthesis reactions aim to produce a single enantiomer, only absolute asymmetric synthesis guarantees 100% enantiomeric excess. This is achieved through highly controlled mechanisms and chiral reagents or catalysts that ensure the formation of only one stereoisomer.
