","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when molecules have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their structure. In the case of C4H10O, there are two types of structural isomerism: chain isomerism and functional group isomerism.

a) Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon skeleton of the molecule is different. For C4H10O, chain isomerism would result in different arrangements of the carbon atoms in the chain. For example, butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-methylpropan-1-ol (CH3CH(OH)CH3) are chain isomers.

b) Functional group isomerism: This occurs when the molecule has the same molecular formula but different functional groups. For C4H10O, this would involve molecules with different functional groups like alcohols (R-OH) and ethers (R-O-R'). For example, butan-1-ol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and diethyl ether (CH3CH2-O-CH2CH3) are functional group isomers.

2. Optical isomerism: This type of isomerism occurs when a molecule has a chiral center, which means it has four different groups attached to a carbon atom. In C4H10O, the molecule 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) has a chiral center at the second carbon atom, and thus exhibits optical isomerism. The two possible optical isomers are called enantiomers, which are non-superimposable mirror images of each other.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism in a molecule with the chemical formula C4H10O are structural isomerism (chain isomerism and functional group isomerism) and optical isomerism.
