<start_of_turn>model
","Let the radius of the cone be \( r \). The lateral surface area \( S \) of the cone is given by:
\[ S = \pi r \), where \( r \) is the slant height. Given \( S = \frac{3}{8}\pi \), we have:
\[ \frac{3}{8}\pi = \pi r \implies r = \frac{3}{8} \]

The unfolded diagram of the cone is a triangle with the slant height as the base and the radius as the apaltitude. The central angle \( \theta \) of the triangle is the angle between the slant height and the radius. Using the sine rule:
\[ \sin \theta = \frac{\text{opposite side}}{\text{hypotenuse}} = \frac{r}{1} = \frac{3}{8} \]
\[ \theta = \sin^{-1} \left( \frac{3}{8} \right) \]
\[ \theta = \boxed{\sin^{-1} \left( \frac{3}{8} \right)} \]
