"Prove that the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, but they do commute if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions in the expressions for \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) and \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) vanish. What are these conditions explicitly?","<bos><start_of_turn>user
Prove that the Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) do not commute in general, but they do commute if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions in the expressions for \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) and \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) vanish. What are these conditions explicitly?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The Riemann-Liouville fractional derivative operators \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) are defined as:

\[
aD_t^p f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t (t - s)^{p-1} f'(s) \, ds, \quad p > 0,
\]

and

\[
aD_t^q f(t) = \frac{1}{a} \int_0^t (t - s)^{q-1} f'(s) \, ds, \quad q > 0,
\]

where \( a \) is a positive constant.

**Non-Commutation:**
For general \( p \neq j \), the operators do not commute because the integrals in their products involve different exponents and integration limits, leading to non-commutation. For example, the product \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) involves an integral of \( (t - s)^{p-1} \) and another of \( (t - s)^{j-1} \), which do not simplify to \( (t - s)^{p+j-1} \) in general.

**Commutation with Special Conditions:**
The operators commute if the sums involving the lower terminal conditions vanish. This occurs when the functions \( f(t) \) satisfy the following conditions:

1. For \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \):
   \[
   \int_0^t (t - s)^{p-1} f'(s) \, ds = 0 \quad \text{for all } t \in [0, a).
   \]

2. For \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \):
   \[
   \int_0^t (t - s)^{q-1} f'(s) \, ds = 0 \quad \text{for all } t \in [0, a).
   \]

These conditions ensure that the lower integral in each case vanishes, simplifying the expressions for \( aD_t^p (aD_t^q f(t)) \) and \( aD_t^q (aD_t^p f(t)) \) to match each each other. Thus, the operators commute.

Thus, the condition for \( aD_t^p \) and \( aD_t^q \) to commute is that the integrals of \( f'(s) \) multiplied by the exponents \( (t - s)^{p-1} \) and \( (t - s)^{q-1} \) vanish at the lower terminal condition \( t = 0 \). 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\int_0^t (t - s)^{p-1} f'(s) \, ds = 0 \quad \text{for all } t \in [0, a), \\
&\int_0^t (t - s)^{q-1} f'(s) \, ds = 0 \quad \text{for all } t \in [0, a).
\end{aligned}
}
