","To determine the reaction times that can be followed, we need to calculate the residence time of the fluid in the flow tube. The residence time is the average time for a fluid element to travel from the mixing chamber to the observation point (1–10 cm downstream).

1. Calculate the cross-sectional area of the tubing:
Diameter = 0.1 mm, so radius = 0.05 mm = 50 μm
Area (A) = πr² = π(50 × 10⁻⁶ m)² ≈ 7.85 × 10⁻8 m²

2. Calculate the flow velocity (v):
Flow rate (Q) = 10 cm³ min⁻¹ = 10 × 10⁻6 m³ min⁻¹
v = Q / A = (10 × 10⁻⁶ m³ min⁻¹) / (7.85 × 10⁻⁸ m²) = 127.12 cm/min = 0.2119 min/s

3. Calculate the residence time (τ) for different distances (L) from the mixing chamber:
τ = L / v

