The contribution to the tensile yield strength \(\sigma_{yr}\) due to hard particles is given by the Overthard strengthening equation:

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{G b^2}{d}
\]

where:
- \(\sigma_{yr0}\) is the yield strength of the alloy without particles,
- \(G\) is the shear modulus,
- \(b\) is the Burgers vector,
- \(d\) is the center-to-center spacing of the particles.

Substituting the given values:
\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \text{ GN m}^{-2} \times (0.286 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m})^2}{6 \times 10^{-8} \text{ m}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 10^9 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-18}}{6 \times 10^{-8}}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-1} \text{ GN m}^{-2}}{6}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + \frac{26 \times 0.286^2 \times 10^{-1}}{6} \text{ GN m}^{-2}
\]

\[
\sigma_{yr} = \sigma_{yr0} + 2.3 \text{ GN m}^{-2}
\]

Thus, the contribution of the hard particles to the tensile yield strength is \(\boxed{2.3 \text{ GN m}^{-2}}\).

