","The net chemical reaction during the discharging of a lead storage battery is:
\[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

**Explanation**:
- The lead electrode (anode) is oxidized to form lead sulfate (\(\text{PbSO}_4\)).
- The lead dioxide electrode (cathode) is reduced to form lead sulfate (\(\text{PbSO}_4\)).
- The sulfate ions (\(\text{SO}_4^{2-}\)) and hydrogen ions (\(\text{H}^+\)) from the electrolyte combine to form water (\(\text{H}_2\text{O}\)).
- The overall reaction is:
  \[ \text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)} \]

Thus, the lead sulfate (\(\text{PbSO}_4\)) precipitates out of the solution, and the battery stops discharging. 

\boxed{\text{Pb(s)} + \text{PbO}_2(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}^+(aq) + 2\text{SO}_4^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow 2\text{PbSO}_4(\text{s}) + 2\text{H}_2\text{O(l)}}
