For a mole of \(^4\text{He}\) at 1 bar and 290 K, determine the thermal de Broglie wavelength \(\lambda\), the fugacity \(\exp(\mu/kT)\), the free enthalpy (in J), and the entropy (in J/K). Here, helium may be taken as an ideal gas.<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Thermal de Broglie Wavelength (\(\lambda\))**:
   The thermal de Broglie wavelength is given by:
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{h}{\sqrt{2\pi m kT}}
   \]
   Substituting the values:
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{\sqrt{2 \times \pi \times 4 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} \text{ kg} \times 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K} \times 290 \text{ K}}}
   \]
   \[
   \lambda = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{1.16 \times 10^{-26} \text{ kg m/s}} = 4..8 \times 10^{-7} \text{ m} = 480 \text{ nm}
   \]

2. **Fugacity (\(\exp(\mu/kT)\))**:
   For an ideal gas, the fugacity is equal to the pressure:
   \[
   \exp(\mu/kT) = p = 1 \text{ bar} = 10^5 \text{ Pa}
   \]

3. **Free Enthalpy (\(\mu\))**:
   The chemical potential \(\mu\) is related to the fugacity by:
   \[
   \mu = kT \ln \left( \frac{p}{p^0} \right)
   \]
   For an ideal gas, the standard pressure \(p^0\) is 1 atm (101.325 kPa). Thus:
   \[
   \mu = 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K} \times 290 \text{ K} \times \ln \left( \frac{10^5 \text{ Pa}}{101.325 \times 10^3 \text{ Pa}} \right)
