1. As matter and radiation approach the event horizon, they are being pulled away by the extreme gravitational force of the black hole. This causes the matter to accelerate and increase its speed, while the radiation's wavelength shortens due to the Doppler effect.

2. As matter and radiation approach the event horizon, they are stretched and compressed by the increasing gravitational gradient, a phenomenon known as the ""Graham-Nordall effect."" This stretching effect can cause the matter to become a ""streamer"" of sorts, with its one end closer to the black hole and the other end still outside the event horizon.

