","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a photon scattered by an electron is given by the Compton effect formula:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta \lambda\) is the change in wavelength,  
- \(\hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.0545 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s}\)),  
- \(m_e\) is the electron mass (\(9.109 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg}\)),  
- \(c\) is the speed of light (\(2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}\)),  
- \(\theta\) is the scattering angle.  

The maximum wavelength change occurs when \(\theta = 180^\circ\) (i.e., the electron is scattered head-on), so:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]  
The percentage change is then:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]  
Substituting the values:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{2 \pi \times 1.0545 \times 10^{-34}}{9.109 \times 10^{-31} \times 2.998 \times 10^8} \right) \times \frac{1}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100
\]  
\[
\text{Percentage change} \approx 0.00035 \, \text{or} \, 0.035\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is \(\boxed{0.035\%}\).
